gile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is similar to the typical Psychology paper (median shown for reference); relative to other words, papers with <span  \"><i>\"higherorder\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">82<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability(see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">Supplemental Materials 7.3 of the associated paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"higherorder\" </i></span> tend to be published in lower impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">16<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  and tend to be cited less (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">17<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"higherorder\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">38<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}